WEBVTT

1
00:00:00.000 --> 00:00:01.877
Matching settings to task types.

2
00:00:02.157 --> 00:00:10.627
No public benchmark cleanly ranks today’s models by task type, and claims that “model X is best at refactoring” are mostly anecdote.

3
00:00:10.907 --> 00:00:14.576
What you can do is reason from what each task demands, then test.

4
00:00:14.856 --> 00:00:16.861
Here are defensible starting points.

5
00:00:17.141 --> 00:00:19.211
Large refactors and migrations.

6
00:00:19.491 --> 00:00:23.501
The risk is missing a caller or breaking a contract across many files.

7
00:00:23.781 --> 00:00:35.707
Start with the strongest model at high effort, plan first (lesson 6), and insist on a check that covers the whole surface (the full test suite, a typecheck, a grep that must come back empty).

8
00:00:35.987 --> 00:00:45.224
For many mechanical, independent edits, fan-out beats one long session: Claude Code’s /batch splits a change into units in separate worktrees.

9
00:00:45.504 --> 00:00:51.157
Per-unit work is often fine on a cheaper model; review sampling matters more than model choice there.

10
00:00:51.437 --> 00:00:52.973
Greenfield prototypes.

11
00:00:53.253 --> 00:00:56.219
The risk is building the wrong thing, not subtle bugs.

12
00:00:56.499 --> 00:00:59.848
Lower effort and fast iteration are fine while you steer.

13
00:01:00.128 --> 00:01:09.280
The Claude Code docs note that Claude tends to build the smallest thing that satisfies a request, and that saying “be ambitious” tells it about scope you want.

14
00:01:09.560 --> 00:01:21.848
Prototypes are also where you can let an agent run most freely, because the code is meant to be thrown away; the danger is a prototype quietly becoming production code without the review it never got.

15
00:01:22.128 --> 00:01:22.960
Debugging.

16
00:01:23.240 --> 00:01:30.792
Agents are strong here when the bug is reproducible, because the loop has a check: reproduce, hypothesize, change, rerun.

17
00:01:31.072 --> 00:01:43.872
Simon Willison lists debugging among the problems that suit agentic loops for exactly this reason, alongside performance tuning and dependency upgrades: clear success criteria plus trial and error.

18
00:01:44.152 --> 00:01:47.053
Raise effort for unfamiliar or intermittent bugs.

19
00:01:47.333 --> 00:01:52.837
If you can’t reproduce the bug, the agent can’t either; spend your effort on a reproduction first.

20
00:01:53.117 --> 00:01:54.035
Code review.

21
00:01:54.315 --> 00:02:02.101
The model matters less than independence: a reviewer that didn’t write the code and didn’t see the reasoning that produced it (lesson 8).

22
00:02:02.381 --> 00:02:06.627
A different model family can add independence, at the cost of more noise.

23
00:02:06.907 --> 00:02:08.528
Data work and analysis.

24
00:02:08.808 --> 00:02:13.800
Correctness is hard to see, because a wrong join still produces a plausible table.

25
00:02:14.080 --> 00:02:22.528
Prefer settings that make the agent show its work (queries, row counts, sanity checks), and verify the numbers against something you trust.

26
00:02:22.808 --> 00:02:24.408
Effort helps less than checks.

27
00:02:24.688 --> 00:02:38.704
Across all of them, METR’s time-horizon research is the best general guide to scope: success falls as tasks get longer, so a task you can break into checkable pieces is a task you can delegate more safely, whatever the model.
